{"text": "city of\nCINCINNATI\nMarch 3, 2020\nFOR YOUR INFORMATION\nTo: Mayor and Members of Council\nG7\n\nSubject: City of Cincinnati Efforts to Address the Coronavirus\n\nFrom: Patrick A. Duhaney, City Manager\n\nThe purpose of this memo is to provide an update on efforts by the City of Cincinnati to address\nconcems related to a novel (new) coronavirus, otherwise known as \u201cCOVID-19.\"\n\nBackground on Disease and Current Status\nThe outbreak started in Wuhan, China, but cases have been identified in a growing number of other\ninternational locations, including the United States. The Centers of Disease Control and Prevention\n(CDC) has issued travel guidance for those traveling to the following countries/locations: China, Hong\nKong, Iran, Italy, Japan and South Korea. The CDC also recommends that all travelers reconsider cruise\nship voyages into or within Asia at this time.\n\nDuring the week of February 23, the CDC reported community spread of the virus that causes COVID-19\nin two locations in California, as well as Oregon and Washington. Community spread in Washington\nresulted in the first death in the U.S. from COVID-19, according to the CDC.\n\nThe State of Ohio has had several patients under investigation for the disease; however, none of these\ncases tested positive as the coronavirus. With that said, the CDC warns that more cases of COVID-19\nare likely to be identified in the United States, and it is possible that at some point widespread\ntransmission of the disease may occur within this country.\n\nThe CDC is currently working to fully deploy a test for the pathogen and to equip state and local public\nhealth labs with testing capacity. Currently test kits have been deployed to the State Health Department\nLaboratory but not to any commercial laboratories. Local health departments do not have access to the\ntest kits.\n\nPreparedness\nThe Cincinnati Health Department (CHD) and our emergency response team (led by the Cincinnati Fire\nDepartment) train and prepare year-round for emergency incidents including participating in regular\nmulti-agency emergency exercises. For several weeks the group has been working closely with the\nCDC, state and regional health agencies, the Red Cross, and other City departments to come up with an\napproach plan. This includes daily phone calls for sharing operational and situational updates for\nplanning purposes. The City is also coordinating with local organizations such as universities and\nindustries whose employees travel internationally or whose clients visit from across the globe.\n\nCurrently, the City\u2019s plan includes both awareness and educational outreach as well as the\nestablishment of reporting processes for possible cases or contaminations. Staff at the Emergency\n\u2018Communications Center are being trained on questions to ask in appropriate circumstances to identify if\nan individual may be experiencing symptoms linked to COVID-19 and to relay that information to first\nresponders.",
